-- title:
--   Restricted 𝒪_X(Cᵢ) raises the Čech Euler characteristic by r
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be a field, $X$ a scheme, and $I,J$ two quasi-coherent ideal sheaf data on $X$. Assume $I$ is invertible in the sense that every point of $X$ lies in a basic open $X.\mathrm{basicOpen}\,f$ for some affine open $U$ and $f\in\Gamma(X,U)$ such that $I$ evaluated on the affine basic open of $f$ is the ideal generated by a single non-zero-divisor. Let $y\colon V(J)\to\operatorname{Spec} k$ be a proper morphism from the closed subscheme cut out by $J$, and assume the inverse-image ideal $I.\mathrm{comap}$ along the closed immersion $V(J)\hookrightarrow X$ is invertible in the same sense. Let $r$ be a natural number, assume the composite of the closed immersion of $V(I.\mathrm{comap})$ into $V(J)$ with $y$ is finite, and that its fibre rank at every point of $\operatorname{Spec} k$ equals $r$. Finally let $\mathcal{W}$ be a two-chart affine cover of $V(J)$: two opens $U_0,U_1$, both affine with affine intersection, covering $V(J)$. Writing $\chi(M)=\dim_k H^0-\dim_k H^1$ for the two-chart Čech complex of $M$ associated with $\mathcal{W}$ and $y$, whose $H^0$ is the kernel and $H^1$ the cokernel of $(m_0,m_1)\mapsto -m_0|_{U_0\cap U_1}+m_1|_{U_0\cap U_1}$ on $\Gamma(M,U_0)\times\Gamma(M,U_1)\to\Gamma(M,U_0\cap U_1)$, the conclusion is the identity in $\mathbb{Z}$ $$\chi\bigl(\text{pullback along }V(J)\hookrightarrow X\text{ of }\mathcal{H}om(I,\mathcal{O}_X)\bigr)=\chi(\mathcal{O}_{V(J)})+r,$$ where $\mathcal{H}om(I,\mathcal{O}_X)$ is the dual of the module associated with $I$ and $\mathcal{O}_{V(J)}$ is the monoidal unit of the category of modules on $V(J)$.
--
--   This is the statement that, for effective Cartier divisors $C_i=V(I)$ and $C_j=V(J)$ with $C_j$ proper over $k$, the intersection number $(C_i\cdot C_j)$ read as the length $r$ of $C_i\cap C_j$ measures the jump in Euler characteristic when $\mathcal{O}_{C_j}$ is replaced by $\mathcal{O}_X(C_i)|_{C_j}$, in the two-chart Čech formulation of cohomology used throughout. It feeds the Euler-characteristic bookkeeping for resolved models of modular curves, being cited in the Deligne–Rapoport resolved model package.
-- source:
